Switchgear lets operators connect, disconnect and isolate electrical circuits. Protective relays detect abnormal conditions and can command circuit breakers to interrupt fault currents. Opening a breaker may be the correct response to a fault; allowing current to continue could damage equipment.

The engineering challenge is to disconnect the affected section while preserving healthy sections where the design permits. This coordination matters: protection that isolates too much can turn a local problem into a larger outage.

Isolation also permits maintenance. Reliability includes the ability to work on equipment without losing the critical load, when the architecture is designed for that requirement. A second cable at the rack does not establish that capability.

In our opening incident, both rack feeds lose their upstream source when the common breaker opens. The failure is in what the overall architecture can tolerate, even if the breaker itself operates correctly.

The first seconds after power disappears

Computers cannot simply wait while an engine starts. A UPS uses stored energy to bridge interruptions and, depending on its design, condition the power supplied to critical equipment. Batteries are common, though other storage approaches exist.

A generator has a different role: it can provide longer support once running and connected, subject to fuel, capacity and operating limits. Transfer controls manage the change of source. The UPS buys time for that sequence or for a controlled shutdown.

Stored energy → UPS supports the load while another source becomes ready

Generator ready → transfer equipment connects it to a usable electrical path

Neither mechanism guarantees survival of every fault. A charged battery cannot deliver power through a disconnected downstream circuit. A generator cannot repair a failed common switchboard merely by starting. Placement and connections matter as much as equipment names.

Ask what survives, not what is duplicated

Return to the A and B feeds. Suppose their upstream paths are now independent, but either surviving path can carry only half the rack’s full demand. Is the problem solved?

Answer: Only partly. Independence removes the shared failure, but the surviving path must also have sufficient capacity for the required load. Otherwise the operator must reduce load or lose equipment after a failure.

Redundancy must be assessed against a specific event: loss of a supply module, a distribution path, utility power, or equipment taken out for maintenance. One design need not survive all of these. Shared controls or physical hazards can also defeat apparent duplication.

This echoes cluster reliability: useful capacity is what remains available under the conditions that matter, not a count of installed components.

Why a 100 MW announcement needs a second question

Imagine a site permitted to draw 100 megawatts from the utility. One megawatt is 1,000 kilowatts. Its installed electrical path can currently deliver only 70 MW to the data halls. These are teaching assumptions, not a named project.

Can it operate 100 MW of IT equipment? No. The usable limit is 70 MW or less at that point. Other electrical limits, redundancy reservations and cooling may reduce usable IT capacity further. Building overhead must also be accounted for at the relevant measurement boundary.

Now imagine the operator orders GPUs with another 30 MW of electrical demand. A purchase order changes committed spending; delivery increases equipment inventory. Neither, by itself, increases energized compute. The missing electrical capacity must be installed, integrated and tested, with adequate cooling, before the additional hardware becomes useful.

That is the supply-chain consequence. Transformers, switchgear, UPS equipment, batteries, generators, busways and cables must form a compatible system. A missing component or a failed acceptance test can delay the value of much more expensive computing equipment.

